  "image_name": "d47e6fc9f6459cc2c5b3100f888110f3.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares food-web structure across three Arctic marine settings: the southern Barents Sea, the northern Barents Sea under “borealization,” and the northern Barents Sea/Eurasian Basin farther into the Arctic. It shows a clear shift from a warmer, more fish-rich shelf system to a colder, deeper Arctic system with fewer pelagic fish and more ice-associated and deep-water organisms.\n\nIn the southern Barents Sea, the scene is dominated by cod, capelin, and other fish in midwater, with zooplankton near the surface and benthic invertebrates on the seafloor. In the borealized northern Barents Sea, many of these southern fish appear farther north, suggesting an expansion of boreal species into Arctic waters. The upper layer also seems influenced by sea ice, while zooplankton, jellyfish, and benthic fauna remain present.\n\nThe right-hand panel extends this gradient into the Eurasian Basin, where the water column is deeper and the fish community looks sparser. Larger copepods and a few fish occupy the upper and midwater, while the seabed supports corals, sea lilies, stars, brittle stars, and crabs. Overall, the image highlights a northward transition in species composition and habitat structure, emphasizing how borealization alters Arctic marine ecosystems.",
